{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic","id":1044811,"title":"Do people use Abilities Enhancing Magic every single time they use magic?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-02-06T18:45:34-08:00","id":1044811,"title":"Do people use Abilities Enhancing Magic every single time they use magic?","content":"Science is an Ability. Computer is an Ability. There are two Traditions whose magic is almost entirely focused through those things, respectively. So why wouldn't they get that bonus every time? For the seven other Traditions, especially the Order of Hermes, there is Occult. Is there any mystic magic, particularly ritual magic, to which Occult does not apply? This seems incredibly broken to me--not to mention cumbersome, with all the additional rolling as players just make Ability rolls part of their magic routine. Have any you played or seen play where Abilities Enhancing Magic is just routine?<br /> <br /> This is a base rule but nonetheless seems like a terrible and utterly transformative one that should be discarded. Magic Enhancing Abilities, however, I have no problem with. There you actually have to come up with a justification for how the magic enhances the ability.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 02-06-2017, 10:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-06T20:52:13-08:00","id":1044870,"title":"","content":"We use it in my games from time to time. It's very much a context based thing, and the players normally don't unless I offer it up to them.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking, if only roll abilities when their knowledge or skill is particularly pertinent. For example, if my VA wanted to make an Entropy 3 chaos virus, I might let them roll wits and computer to demonstrate the virus bit.<br /> If I had someone who danced as their focus, and try were trying to use a mind effect to distract people, a performance roll might come into play.<br /> This would especially work if their speciality came into play. If I had a verbena who had occult specialising in herblore, then if they tried to make a craft a quick balm to heal some damage, this could come into play.<br /> <br /> It doesn't happen often, so it never becomes routine.<br /> <br /> Most of the time all this will do is drop the difficulty of the ar\u00eate roll, but sometimes it results in being able to do the spell at all. If one of my mages wants to use forces to activate an electric lock, they'ed have to roll intelligence and Technology to determine if they knew what they were doing. Similar for certain kinds of Life effects and medicine rolls.<br /> <br /> I also use the rank level itself as an indicator of ability. If the VA had only one dot in computers, first of all what the hell is wrong with them, but second, I'd make them roll computer every time they wanted to do something more advanced than opening a window, I'd be more forgiving at 2, and wouldn't require it at 3 or higher.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-06T21:22:38-08:00","id":1044879,"title":"","content":"That is a good idea, requiring that the Abilities be really pertinent to the Effect. Especially if the kind of Effect they're doing is considered rare and powerful within the Paradigm. Or for ritual, it could be certain Rotes that for which ritual precision is extra important. Maybe it's also a good idea, if allowing it, to straight up tell players that one does not expect the rule to be used much and the requirements for using it are stringent.<br /> <br /> I also use the Ability minima for stuff. If an Effect is reliant on an Ability, I usually say they can't roll more Arete dice or use a higher Sphere than they have dots in that Ability (not including added Attribute)--unless the Ability is at 5 and their Arete is higher than 5.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-06T22:16:19-08:00","id":1044904,"title":"","content":"Don't forget that, at least in M20, there's the optional rule that if a mage's focus/instrument is necessarily tied to a given ability (like for instance, Firearms) that ability rating must at least equal the sphere rating in use in order for the mage to employ an Effect without transcending foci.<br /> <br /> Also, don't forget the Occult skill is <i>dramatically </i>different in M20 than earlier versions, being a catch-all skill reflecting <i>Sleeper</i> knowledge of the occult, opposed to a catch-all skill reflecting <i>any</i> knowledge of the occult. To have the focused, guaranteed-to-be-accurate, sort of knowledge that goes along with focus and instrument use, a mage needs the relevant Esoterica specialty rather than Occult. That aforementioned Verbena would need <i>Esoterica (herbalism)</i><i>, </i>or maybe even <i>Medicine</i>, but <i>not</i> Occult.<br /> <br /> That aside, don't forget the minimum difficulty of a given Arete roll is -3 from the default difficulty (to an absolute basement of difficulty 3). There are plenty of ways to reduce difficulty aside from Abilities Enhancing Magick; and to be honest, there's no reason a given mage whose skills well exceed the minimum to use an Effect, shouldn't have a bonus creating that Effect.<br /> <br /> That said, typically I only allow it when there's an instrument in play necessarily tied to a given ability. &quot;I know this blanket ability, so I should have a bonus every time I use magick&quot; doesn't fly by me, nor should it anyone else really.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 02-07-2017, 01:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T06:48:10-08:00","id":1045033,"title":"","content":"Our group loves the Ability enhances Magick and visa versa rules. They sound slightly abusive on paper I'll admit but they aren't. If anything it helps with focus / instrument selections with new players. Also remember that doing so takes extra time which isn't alway avaliable. Especially in combat where unless your combat ability or weapon of choice is your focus it takes multiple actions to perform in a single turn. <br /> <br /> As for abilities enhancing magick I often rule that it automatically puts the Arete Roll into requiring extra time (not extended roll) without the -1 bonus. I also feel this section of rules encourage players to better think out how their magick is an extension of their belief put into action. Which makes for better Role Playing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T08:38:58-08:00","id":1045090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tuch</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045033#post1045033\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...Also remember that doing so takes extra time which isn't alway avaliable. Especially in combat where unless your combat ability or weapon of choice is your focus it takes multiple actions to perform in a single turn...</div> </div> </div> </div>This is actually one part of the rules with which I firmly disagree. I know it's there for game balance sake, but on the other hand broken down logically it's contrary as hell. Higher dot ratings don't just imply a broader knowledge base with the relevant ability, and/or a deeper knowledge base; it implies <i>fluency</i> with that relevant ability.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, this reflects itself in higher dot ratings allowing (from a statistics perspective) dramatic success more often, which may reduce time needed to accomplish a given task, and allowing success on extended rolls which fewer rolls made (and thereby, less time). Moreover, this bridges the minor issue that arises when players have high attribute ratings but low ability ratings, versus players with low attribute ratings but high ability ratings, but identical dice pools (or +/-1 dice pools); the former has raw talent for that skill, the latter has <i>fluency</i> in that skill, that allow them to perform on a comparable level.<br /> <br /> Case in point, VA's/ME's as mentioned earlier in this thread. The Computer skill is good and all, but in M20 it actually <i>doesn't</i> encompass a broad or deep enough knowledge base VA's in particular would need to do complex magick (assuming their focus is reality hacking). What does, is Science (computer science); why this isn't made clearer in the core book, and the writers resorted to the older version language of Computer = God-Skill, is beyond me. Maybe I just have a broad enough knowledge of IT myself to know the difference. At my tables, I wouldn't allow a VA with Computer out the wazoo, but nothing or very little in Science (computer science) to accomplish a whole lot.<br /> <br /> But, I digress. You gotta know code languages, to code. And, like any other language, the more practiced you are and the more you know, the more <i>fluent</i> you are, which means you can code faster, cleaner, and with fewer bugs in the end product. Which means your ability to produce on the clock or under pressure is going to be greater than someone with less experience and a smaller, shallower knowledge base. That should mechanically represent itself somehow, and abilities enhancing magick is an excellent outlet for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T09:07:17-08:00","id":1045121,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a> Wait, so if coding has nothing to do with the Computer skill, what does 5 points of Computer let you do?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T09:27:09-08:00","id":1045147,"title":"","content":"I think there is a hidden question hidden here. What do the VAs think they are hacking into? Why do they think code on a computer can change reality? It is easy to say reality hacking, but harder to define it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T10:26:14-08:00","id":1045206,"title":"","content":"I'll expand on that using the Matrix as an easy example.<br /> <br /> Lets say a VA wants to make it daytime. Now there's a fairly good chance that he just has to change the system clock to noon. If he knows where those functions are he can do that. There might also be a subroutine the machines use to change the time. Computers.<br /> <br /> However, lets say he wants to cause a small thunderstorm inside of a hotel lobby. It's exceptionally unlikely that there is a setting like HiltonLobby.Thunderstorm=False; that he can change. Instead he'll have to change a variety of other variables like incoming air temperature, humidity, coefficient of friction, etc that will interact in the Matrix in such a way as to crate the desired indoor weather. Science.<br /> <br /> So which skill you use depends a great deal on how you think reality is structured and how you are going about changing it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 02-07-2017, 01:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T11:25:45-08:00","id":1045241,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Case in point, VA's/ME's as mentioned earlier in this thread. The Computer skill is good and all, but in M20 it actually <i>doesn't</i> encompass a broad or deep enough knowledge base VA's in particular would need to do complex magick (assuming their focus is reality hacking). What does, is Science (computer science); why this isn't made clearer in the core book, and the writers resorted to the older version language of Computer = God-Skill, is beyond me. Maybe I just have a broad enough knowledge of IT myself to know the difference. At my tables, I wouldn't allow a VA with Computer out the wazoo, but nothing or very little in Science (computer science) to accomplish a whole lot.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would think that high level Computers should cover Computer Science, in the same way that Medicine should cover Medical Science, and not just first aid stuff. Ain't that how things work?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T11:43:15-08:00","id":1045255,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045121#post1045121\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a> Wait, so if coding has nothing to do with the Computer skill, what does 5 points of Computer let you do?</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't say it coding has &quot;nothing&quot; to do with the Computer skill, be careful with your own inferences, there. Case in point, the write-up for the Computer skill, on page 283 of the M20 corebook (emphasis mine):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 corebook, page 283</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This knowledge reflects your understanding of Information Technology; a single dot involves basic computer use (whatever that looks like in your chronicle), whereas higher ratings show a deeper understanding of hardware, software, interface, the Internet, and IT culture in general. <i>Certain tasks (like advanced programming or repair) and systems (especially the arcane tech employed by Technomancers) demand <b>at least</b> three or four dots in this Trait <b>before your character can even begin to understand them</b></i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Computer has the same relationship with Science (computer science) as Technology does with Science (relevant field). Which is the same relationship, in earlier versions, that Occult had with Lore (relevant specialty); a broad, jack-of-all-trades understanding, specifically of <i>application</i>, which does not necessarily entail a deep understanding of any given aspect of its purview.<br /> <br /> What do you get with Computer, alone? Script kiddie shit. Simpler programming languages: Java, VB, HTML. Maybe a basic grasp of more advanced stuff like Python and C/C++. More than that, and you have to have a fairly broad background understanding of information theory, network theory, symbolic logic, advanced mathematics, complexity theory, even quantum mechanics and chaos theory (especially when Technomancers are involved). None of which the Computer skill provides.<br /> <br /> <i>That's</i> where Science (computer science) comes in, because it provides the background theoretical knowledge necessary to do more advanced and complex shit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What do the VAs think they are hacking into? Why do they think code on a computer can change reality...I'll expand on that using the Matrix as an easy example. </div> </div> </div>Basically, yes, it's the Matrix. They believe in the simulation hypothesis, and all they're doing is process tweaking and management during run-time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T11:57:46-08:00","id":1045270,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a> - I think you're being excessively strict to your players and pretty elitist about programming languages. As far as I'm aware almost everyone else plays Computer as being sufficient for <b>all </b>programming tasks. <b>Requiring </b>a player to take additional dots in another skill to support them in the first skill is just disempowering them.<br /> <br /> You <i>might </i>want Science (computer science) if you are inventing something like a Wonder but not for general programming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T12:09:22-08:00","id":1045279,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045241#post1045241\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would think that high level Computers should cover Computer Science, in the same way that Medicine should cover Medical Science, and not just first aid stuff. Ain't that how things work?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, no. Medicine is the diagnosis and treatment of medical problems. A high Medicine skill is going to give you the ability to read a PET scan and figure out from the PET scan what may or may be wrong with a patient, the risk factors associated with administering a PET scan, and an <b><i>extremely</i></b><i><b> basic</b></i> understanding of how a PET scan works.<br /> <br /> How to <i>perform</i> a PET scan, no, not necessarily; that's a lower level of Medicine (enough to administer dyes and monitor patients through the process), Technology, and Computer. Oncologists aren't nuclear medicine techs, and vice versa. How to build or engineer a PET scanner, research potential applications for PET scanning technology, or improve upon PET scanning technology, <i>oh hell to the no</i>.<br /> <br /> What <i>will</i> happen, on the other hand, is doctors who employ and rely on nuclear medicine on a regular basis, for instance, are going to pick up a dot or two in Science (physics), and Technology, to get a more in-depth understanding of the tools available for use in diagnosis and treatment. Whereas medical research scientists may only have 2-3 dots of Medicine proper, but are going to go whole hog on the Sciences and Technology, because their focus lies elsewhere from diagnosis and treatment.<br /> <br /> I'll use <i>House</i> as an example. Dr. House as a diagnostician has a fuckload of dots in Enigmas, and a bunch of Sciences on the side; probably even a couple dots of Esotericas, given the weirdo shit he sees. The other doctors around him (his team) may have the same number of Enigmas dots, maybe even a diagnostics specialty, but <i>not</i> the accompanying knowledges he has (which is why they're inferior diagnosticians, and there to <i>learn</i> from Dr. House). Dr. Wilson, on the other hand, is an oncologist and as such doesn't need the wide array of Sciences and Enigmas Dr. House nor his team needs, because he's not a diagnostician; what Wilson <i>will</i> have on the other hand, as an oncologist is a higher Science (physics) rating than Dr. House or his team, because he deals extensively with nuclear medicine being an oncologist.<br /> <br /> And of all of them, they all have Medicine ratings of 4+ (5 in the case of House and Wilson). It's those ancillary skills (and skill specialties) which are the source of cross-disciplinary knowledge and expertise in a given field.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T12:34:07-08:00","id":1045301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045270#post1045270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a> - I think you're being excessively strict to your players and pretty elitist about programming languages. As far as I'm aware almost everyone else plays Computer as being sufficient for <b>all </b>programming tasks. <b>Requiring </b>a player to take additional dots in another skill to support them in the first skill is just disempowering them.<br /> <br /> You <i>might </i>want Science (computer science) if you are inventing something like a Wonder but not for general programming.</div> </div> </div> </div>That would be because the Computers skill (among others) was only changed in M20, like many other Knowledges that had proven problematic in previous editions (see, Occult), and that information is easy to overlook, especially among players and ST's unused to such fundamental changes between versions who may not even pay attention to individual ability write-ups for such. Doubly so for skills like Computers, when the last edition was released in '99, and computer use and IT have seen seismic shifts since.<br /> <br /> Beyond that...<br /> <br /> In your opinion. Personally, if a player brings to me a character sheet with 4+ in Computers and nothing in Science (computer science), the very first thing I'm doing is asking them where and how they got that education without being introduced, at any point along the way, to rudimentary computer science. Just like if someone brings a character sheet with 4+ Esoterica (yoga) yet somehow few if any dots in Athletics, or a monstrous Drive score and nothing in Crafts (automotive/vehicle repair).<br /> <br /> Because it makes zero sense, whatsoever, for PC's to have learned a given abilty to a professional level, or to mastery, while having picked up <i>no</i> cross-disciplinary or related knowledge. And, frankly, bringing such a PC to a table is a sure sign of not having put too much thought into the character, let alone enough to consistently or effectively play a paradigm, or looking to twink; neither of which are traits I seek to encourage among my players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T12:53:12-08:00","id":1045320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045301#post1045301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That would be because the Computers skill (among others) was only changed in M20, like many other Knowledges that had proven problematic in previous editions (see, Occult), and that information is easy to overlook, especially among players and ST's unused to such fundamental changes between versions who may not even pay attention to individual ability write-ups for such. Doubly so for skills like Computers, when the last edition was released in '99, and computer use and IT have seen seismic shifts since.<br /> <br /> Beyond that...<br /> <br /> In your opinion. Personally, if a player brings to me a character sheet with 4+ in Computers and nothing in Science (computer science), the very first thing I'm doing is asking them where and how they got that education without being introduced, at any point along the way, to rudimentary computer science. Just like if someone brings a character sheet with 4+ Esoterica (yoga) yet somehow few if any dots in Athletics, or a monstrous Drive score and nothing in Crafts (automotive/vehicle repair).<br /> <br /> Because it makes zero sense, whatsoever, for PC's to have learned a given abilty to a professional level, or to mastery, while having picked up <i>no</i> cross-disciplinary or related knowledge. And, frankly, bringing such a PC to a table is a sure sign of not having put too much thought into the character, let alone enough to consistently or effectively play a paradigm, or looking to twink; neither of which are traits I seek to encourage among my players.</div> </div> </div> </div>That just feels like you're imposing a skill tax of sorts. The whole point of WoD's ST style games was to get away from D&amp;D style Skill Trees like this.<br /> <br /> I'd say if I had someone with Computer 4 but no Science, or Science 4 (computer) but no computer, I'd rule that the former had a practical understanding of the subject, while the latter had a theoretical understanding.<br /> <br /> It would be the same if someone had Performance (Music) but nothing in Academics (Music Theory), or vise versa.<br /> <br /> When it came time to roll about something relevant, I would allow both these character to make a roll, they are approaching the issue from different sides, and as a result may produce differing results.<br /> <br /> I know a lot of Doctors that have effectively no understanding of molecular biology, similarly I know a lot of Biomedical Scientists who couldn't do first aid to save their life (literally).<br /> You don't need to be athletic to do yoga, you could just be very flexible or practised. You don't need to know the inns and outs of your car and how it's built in order to have the reflexes and capability to drive it and drive it incredibly well.<br /> You don't need to understand anything about how computers work in order to be a coding master. It's the old joke, How many Software Engineers does it take to screw in a light bulb? Answer: &quot;Hold on, that's a Hardware problem, not my field&quot;.<br /> <br /> Would you also say that a person can't have Law 4 without Expression?<br /> or Politics 4 without Subterfuge?<br /> or Science (astronomy) without Cosmology?<br /> <br /> Why?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":12193,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","user":"Saikou","date":". 02-12-2017, 05:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T17:17:46-08:00","id":1045511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045320#post1045320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That just feels like you're imposing a skill tax of sorts. The whole point of WoD's ST style games was to get away from D&amp;D style Skill Trees like this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course, the whole point of the ST system was to be able to build holistic, organic characters apart from a linear class- or role-based system of progression with arbitrary limits on what can be learned and at what point. This <i>doesn't</i> exclude characters from having holistic, organic knowledge bases of chosen fields of specialty as <i>that is the entire point</i>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd say if I had someone with Computer 4 but no Science, or Science 4 (computer) but no computer, I'd rule that the former had a practical understanding of the subject, while the former had a theoretical understanding. </div> </div> </div>Which is fine. But without that theoretical understanding, they ain't doing advanced coding or networking applications. Which is pretty much what the Computer skill says by RAW. I'd be <b><i>much</i></b> more interested to hear an explanation you have yet to provide in which someone skilled to a professional level in either managed to avoid, entirely, exposure to the other.<br /> <br /> Because, you're seriously suggesting someone with a Ph.D. level of understanding of computer science (4 dots in Science: comp sci), can get that without ever having learned to connect a device to a wireless network and authenticate a connection, access an e-mail server, defrag a hard drive, or crack open a case and clean it (Computers 1). I just want to make sure we're clear on this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It would be the same if someone had Performance (Music) but nothing in Academics (Music Theory), or vise versa. </div> </div> </div>No, it actually wouldn't be the same. Not in the least. They can <i>perform</i> a piece that's already been written, they can't <i>write</i> one.<br /> <br /> Now, what might that tell us about the ability to execute code (Computers), and the ability to <i>write</i> code (Science: comp sci)?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You don't need to be athletic to do yoga, you could just be very flexible or practised. </div> </div> </div>Which is the actual literal definition of the Athletics skill. Athletic ability. Which you have to have in order to do advanced yoga techniques, and will be necessarily developed as you learn.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You don't need to know the inns and outs of your car and how it's built in order to have the reflexes and capability to drive it and drive it incredibly well. </div> </div> </div>Sure, I'll buy that. Drive 4 is the ability to drive at a professional level; race car driver, stunt driver, emergency service drivers, even experienced truckers. In fact, &quot;race car driver&quot; is the example given in the book of someone who would have Drive 4. Now, you are -- without an iota of irony -- expecting me to buy the argument a race car driver, stunt driver, EMS driver, or a semi trucker can get to that level of skill and experience without ever learning how to fix a flat, change their oil, or replace their spark plugs. That's about what Crafts (vehicle repair) 1 gets you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You don't need to understand anything about how computers work in order to be a coding master. </div> </div> </div>Do you want memory leaks that'll bring down a server cluster? Because that's how you get memory leaks that'll bring down a server cluster.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's the old joke, How many Software Engineers does it take to screw in a light bulb? Answer: &quot;Hold on, that's a Hardware problem, not my field&quot;. </div> </div> </div>...an old joke of which you apparently missed the point by far.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Would you also say that a person can't have Law 4 without Expression? </div> </div> </div>Not everyone in the legal field is a litigator. But guess what -- you still have to know at least a bit about litigation, and would have learned about litigation -- along the way. And guess what, even legal scholars still have to publish, deliberate, teach, and/or work the lecture circuit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> or Politics 4 without Subterfuge? </div> </div> </div>Not everyone who studies political science, or has studied political science, even on a professional level, is a politician, elected or appointed officer holder, or candidate for an elected or appointed office. I'm not. But, guess what -- you still have to learn about framing, rhetoric, and public speaking, or will have learned about it along the way, to be able to unpack politicians' statements and communicate back.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 02-07-2017, 08:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T17:45:16-08:00","id":1045522,"title":"","content":"Theodrim, are you saying that the Ability system should actually require players to take correlated Abilities to raise certain other Abilities to a given level?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T17:48:19-08:00","id":1045526,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a> Oh boy... A play-by-play response...<br /> <br /> Ok. You really don't need music theory to compose. There are plenty of composers that don't know the difference between a mixolydian scale and a diminished minor chord, but they can still &quot;feel&quot; the music out. They know what sounds good, they don't really care why it sounds good.<br /> <br /> I could go on about all the other examples, but honestly, it would be rehashing the same point.<br /> <br /> I agree that a lot of these skills are a van-diagram of sorts, and there's a lot of overhang, but to make it a prerequisite is just mean with exp. Why not assume that knowledge is there when they get Computer 4? Why do they suddenly need a point of Science (which they could then use to make rolls about, say, Physics).<br /> <br /> I don't understand what being this strict and anal about it does to enrich the game. Especially since all of it seems based on the whims of the ST.<br /> The spheres are complicated enough, do you really need to rival their complexity with the mundane stats as well?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T18:13:13-08:00","id":1045538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045522#post1045522\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Theodrim, are you saying that the Ability system should actually require players to take correlated Abilities to raise certain other Abilities to a given level?</div> </div> </div> </div>To a certain extent, yeah. Once you go above three dots in a given ability, that represents a professional-level proficiency with that ability, which will invariably be the result of years' worth of education, experience, and practice. Even Talents -- you don't get four dots in Brawl without having done a <i>lot</i> of fighting over the course your life. That's why it's the break point for having specialties, and why PC's can't go above three dots at character generation without freebie expenditure. And honestly, absolutely, over the course of those years' worth of education, experience, and practice you'll have gained exposure and experience in correlative abilities that very well should be reflected on the character sheet.<br /> <br /> Somebody who's done enough fighting over the course of their lives to justify a Brawl rating of 4, will necessarily have learned how to bob and weave, rope-a-dope, and dodge haymakers, strong kicks, and tackles. Moreover, they'll have developed their reflexes, speed, footwork, and chin, and depending upon <i>how </i>they got that experience fighting, they'll <i>probably</i> have had to learn to run away from opponents, bouncers, and the cops. All of which <i>will</i> represent itself in at least <i>some</i> proficiency in Athletics.<br /> <br /> Likewise, they'll have to develop the ability to read an opponent, discern cues and telegraphs, and learn how to preempt and counter moves. So, there's proficiency in Alertness they <i>will</i> have developed over the course of their experience fighting. Then there's Medicine, in the form of knowing how to treat your own wounds, being treated, and seeing others treated especially in an organized environment like being in a boxing club or league, or spending time in a gym.<br /> <br /> Now, I'm not talking more than a dot or two, here, in any given case. You don't need to be an EMT to be a professional boxer. But on the other hand, the idea of being a professional boxer without having learned along the way to becoming a professional boxer, how to treat swelling, minor lacerations, broken noses and ribs, and how to tell the difference between a minor injury through which a person can continue to fight and a potentially serious injury which needs medical attention, is simply laughable.<br /> <br /> This is a part of creating a realistic, organic character. Nine times out of ten, a player acting in good faith is going to bring a character to the table already <i>with</i> those abilities, because to have correlative abilities is just common sense. The tenth time, it's a minor oversight which needs correction and it's not a big deal. At least, that's the way it is at my table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T18:21:41-08:00","id":1045544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1045526#post1045526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[USER=&quot;431&quot;]I agree that a lot of these skills are a van-diagram of sorts, and there's a lot of overhang, but to make it a prerequisite is just mean with exp.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really. There are more than enough experience cost breaks in MtA, and ways around smaller default dice pools, to justify it. Mentor, Library, the Instruction (Teaching, Educating, whatever it was called) skill from 2e, use of the Mind sphere to facilitate learning the same way the Life sphere provides experience cost breaks to raising attributes, grimoires which permanently increase abilities opposed to sphere and/or Arete ratings (which I've allowed in games before), even Effects which temporarily provide bonus dice and/or ability/attribute dots. There's the &quot;magick enhances abilities&quot; rule which stretches smaller dice pools.<br /> <br /> Hell, I've allowed players to roll dice pools of &quot;correlative abilities&quot; at minor (+1-2) difficulty hikes, if that dice pool is higher, <i>[EDIT: or even get a difficulty bonus on the originally-required roll, for having a correlating ability at a high rating] </i>if they can make a persuasive case as to why they should be able to do it. I've allowed Effects which straight-up allow players to sub one attribute or ability for another, if they can make a case for it (like for instance a Mind rote that allows players to roll Wits + Melee instead of Dex + Melee, or Dex + Medicine instead of Dex + Melee).<br /> <br /> You could implement a house rule to allow for experience cost reductions on &quot;correlative abilities&quot;.<br /> <br /> <i>[EDIT: Hell, a few years back I ran a generational Sorcerer's Crusade game, which featured the PC's raising each other's attributes, and teaching each other abilities, at fairly significant experience cost reduction, with the long-term goal of older PC's handing down their skills and expertise to newer generations of mages before they died or moved up within the Order of Reason.]</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 02-07-2017, 09:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-07T21:38:46-08:00","id":1045604,"title":"","content":"I kind of agree with you, but I think this is one reason we have GM's, to enforce common(ish) sense. Making a correlation system would probably be a lot more complicated than the guidance of an attentive ST.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T11:22:24-08:00","id":1045875,"title":"","content":"&gt;But without that theoretical understanding, they ain't doing advanced coding or networking applications.<br /> Okay that is just bollocks.<br /> <br /> I'm a software developer who doesn't have a Computer Science degree and this year I developed a full set of asymmetric encryption code to allow secure communication between a client and a server using public/private keys. As far as your definition of the skills in WoD are concerned what I did was impossible.<br /> <br /> The individual skills in WoD are broad abstractions and each one is pretty much designed to stand on it's own without support from other skills. Forcing players to buy additional skills is, as Saikou put it, a skill tax. You're taking something away from them and that prevents them getting something else.<br /> <br /> e.g. A starting Virtual Adept character forced to take equal dots in Computer and Science (computer science) would probably have to decide between buying level 4 in those skills or an extra sphere/more willpower/etc simply because certain skills are being 'taxed'.<br /> <br /> Are you also requiring people with high Brawl to have Athletics (nearly everyone does anyway but that doesn't mean it's a requirement)? Extend this concept to it's logical extreme and you end up with character classes - all Virtual Adepts must have this exact set of skills, all Void Engineers must have this different set, and all Order of Hermes are exactly the same.<br /> <br /> One of the reasons WoD does so well is that it doesn't force players into creating characters within rigid lines like many other games. Those couple of dots saved by not buying Computer Science can be used to make the character more interesting somewhere else.<br /> <br /> You would be much more realistic giving them free dots in associated skills when they have a high rating in a core skill.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11490,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","user":"Dogstar","date":". 02-08-2017, 02:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-08T11:31:26-08:00","id":1045881,"title":"","content":"I can see the argument that it is a sort of XP tax, but I also think it very much fits into the character of Mage. But in that spirit, I think it should be an ignorable side of the game, and that STs who use it should work to be evenhanded. If VAs need Science and Computers, make sure the other Trads have to diversify a little as well.<br /> <br /> Also, far better to require extra skills based on paradigm than to require extra Spheres based on paradigm. If paradigms have consequences, this is where they should play out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T02:59:14-08:00","id":1047626,"title":"","content":"Yoga 5, Athletics 0? You're great at flexibility and movement on a Yoga mat, when you have 5 minutes to warm up and stretch, doing 1 of the 600 yoga positions you know. However you can't run, climb or dodge since those things happen rapidly and in an uncontrolled environment.<br /> <br /> Does it make more sense for the Yoga Master to have Athletics 1+? Yes, a little bit. But while it's unlikely, I can still see it. And yes, every professional driver will have been shown how to change the oil, but do they necessarily remember how to do it? Especially if they have Retainers. And Computer 5 should be all you need. If someone wants a higher rating, make Computer Science a secondary skill of Computer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T12:09:48-08:00","id":1047803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1047626#post1047626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yoga 5, Athletics 0? You're great at flexibility and movement on a Yoga mat, when you have 5 minutes to warm up and stretch, doing 1 of the 600 yoga positions you know. However you can't run, climb or dodge since those things happen rapidly and in an uncontrolled environment.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is absurd.<br /> <br /> Competitive weightlifting doesn't happen rapidly or in an uncontrolled environment. Would you, for example, bar a character with Athletics (weightlifting), who (obviously) developed that Athletics skill through weightlifting, to add their Athletics pool to dodge, climb, or run? What if that weightlifter played a round of tennis, would you rule their dice in Athletics don't apply? Tennis is a rapid, but fairly controlled, environment which requires a vastly different array of physical abilities and traits than weightlifting.<br /> <br /> How about someone with Athletics (survival training) or Athletics (extreme endurance)? Those are both RAW specialties for endurance, being printed in plain text in the M20 core book. Would you deny their dots in Athletics to dodge?<br /> <br /> No, of course you wouldn't, that's fucking stupid. Because...<br /> <br /> Athletics is, regardless of specialization, an abstraction of a given character's <i>physical fitness</i>. That is to say, reflexes, vestibular and kinesthetic sense, (upper, lower, and core) body strength, flexibility, lung power, fatigue resistance (i.e. endurance), spatial awareness, fine and gross motor skills, within the limits of a character's natural capability (i.e. attributes). Or, to put it a more sensible, accurate, and holistic way taking into account that last point, it's the abstraction of a given character's <i>physical conditioning</i>.<br /> <br /> Which is why it's a standard skill opposed to a specialized skill like Science, Esoterica, Crafts, or Art. And why, at the same time, no matter how counter-intuitive it may seem to someone used to earlier editions of the game, Athletics regardless of specialty is used to dodge.<br /> <br /> Again, you're without a shred of irony suggesting someone can become a &quot;yoga master&quot; without becoming physically fit in the process. A person must be <i>exceptionally</i> fit to do advanced yoga; they have to have excellent vestibular and kinesthetic senses, body strength, lung power, flexibility, and endurance. I've been doing yoga for years off and on, consider myself nothing more than a dabbling amateur, and the most advanced inversion I can do is a supported shoulder stand; even though I know what more advanced inversions are and how to do them, because I'm not physically conditioned enough I can't do them, and if I tried the most likely outcome is physical injury.<br /> <br /> Let's <i>really</i> put this in the ground once and for all. Let's look at Mage, revised edition, which actually had a separate Dodge skill. Let's say someone brought a PC to the table that was an (American) football player, but had zero dodge or brawl.That player wanted to be able to roll Athletics (football) to tackle others and grapple, and dodge hand-to-hand attacks. After all, football is a full-contact sport, played in a rapid and only semi-controlled environment, and dodging, tackling, and grappling are all <i>very</i> necessary parts of playing (American) football.<br /> <br /> Would you allow them to do it, or would you tell them to distribute dots to Brawl and Dodge? Would you buy an &quot;ex-pro football player&quot; character concept with zero Dodge or Brawl? Why or why not? <br /> <br /> Or hell, let's up the ante. Regardless of version, player walks in with a &quot;hockey player&quot; character concept and doesn't have any Brawl or Melee. He wants his character to cross-check an opponent with Forces, using his favorite hockey stick as unique focus/instrument, Would you even consider letting them roll Athletics (hockey) instead of straight Dexterity?<br /> <br /> No, these aren't rhetorical questions, I want you to seriously answer and remain logically consistent with the remarks you've already made, and explain why that wouldn't constitute an &quot;experience tax&quot;, as you put it, on the character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T12:51:45-08:00","id":1047828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1047803#post1047803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is absurd.<br /> <br /> Competitive weightlifting doesn't happen rapidly or in an uncontrolled environment. Would you, for example, bar a character with Athletics (weightlifting), who (obviously) developed that Athletics skill through weightlifting, to add their Athletics pool to dodge, climb, or run? What if that weightlifter played a round of tennis, would you rule their dice in Athletics don't apply? Tennis is a rapid, but fairly controlled, environment which requires a vastly different array of physical abilities and traits than weightlifting.<br /> <br /> How about someone with Athletics (survival training) or Athletics (extreme endurance)? Those are both RAW specialties for endurance, being printed in plain text in the M20 core book. Would you deny their dots in Athletics to dodge?<br /> <br /> No, of course you wouldn't, that's fucking stupid. Because...<br /> <br /> Athletics is, regardless of specialization, an abstraction of a given character's <i>physical fitness</i>. That is to say, reflexes, vestibular and kinesthetic sense, (upper, lower, and core) body strength, flexibility, lung power, fatigue resistance (i.e. endurance), spatial awareness, fine and gross motor skills, within the limits of a character's natural capability (i.e. attributes). Or, to put it a more sensible, accurate, and holistic way taking into account that last point, it's the abstraction of a given character's <i>physical conditioning</i>.<br /> <br /> Which is why it's a standard skill opposed to a specialized skill like Science, Esoterica, Crafts, or Art. And why, at the same time, no matter how counter-intuitive it may seem to someone used to earlier editions of the game, Athletics regardless of specialty is used to dodge.<br /> <br /> Again, you're without a shred of irony suggesting someone can become a &quot;yoga master&quot; without becoming physically fit in the process. A person must be <i>exceptionally</i> fit to do advanced yoga; they have to have excellent vestibular and kinesthetic senses, body strength, lung power, flexibility, and endurance. I've been doing yoga for years off and on, consider myself nothing more than a dabbling amateur, and the most advanced inversion I can do is a supported shoulder stand; even though I know what more advanced inversions are and how to do them, because I'm not physically conditioned enough I can't do them, and if I tried the most likely outcome is physical injury.<br /> <br /> Let's <i>really</i> put this in the ground once and for all. Let's look at Mage, revised edition, which actually had a separate Dodge skill. Let's say someone brought a PC to the table that was an (American) football player, but had zero dodge or brawl.That player wanted to be able to roll Athletics (football) to tackle others and grapple, and dodge hand-to-hand attacks. After all, football is a full-contact sport, played in a rapid and only semi-controlled environment, and dodging, tackling, and grappling are all <i>very</i> necessary parts of playing (American) football.<br /> <br /> Would you allow them to do it, or would you tell them to distribute dots to Brawl and Dodge? Would you buy an &quot;ex-pro football player&quot; character concept with zero Dodge or Brawl? Why or why not?<br /> <br /> Or hell, let's up the ante. Regardless of version, player walks in with a &quot;hockey player&quot; character concept and doesn't have any Brawl or Melee. He wants his character to cross-check an opponent with Forces, using his favorite hockey stick as unique focus/instrument, Would you even consider letting them roll Athletics (hockey) instead of straight Dexterity?<br /> <br /> No, these aren't rhetorical questions, I want you to seriously answer and remain logically consistent with the remarks you've already made, and explain why that wouldn't constitute an &quot;experience tax&quot;, as you put it, on the character.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your physical fitness is a mixture of your Strength, Dexterity, Stamina and Athletics. Athletics is a talent which some people just have, others train it and develop it into a specialized field. Some &quot;athletic&quot; activities exist independent of Athletics since they are suitably distinct. Examples include Melee and Brawl.<br /> <br /> If someone levels up Athletics by training in the Weights room, they are allowed to dodge, climb and run with those dots. Just ask a serious weightlifter to climb something, their strength compared to their weight is phenomenal, and they've trained to control their bodyweight. They'd do better if they had the Secondary Ability (the system I use) Climb, since that would increase the size of their dicepool considerably, but they can climb. Same goes for dodging and running, since they have trained to move explosively and carry their strength in precise ways.<br /> <br /> In the case of Tennis, I would argue that it is specialized enough that they could use their Athletics but at +2 difficulty until they had spent some time playing Tennis and learned the basics. Some common sense may be used.<br /> <br /> All of the types of Athletics you're listing involve explosive movement which MUST improve your physical fitness and your ability to move quickly in demanding and varied ways. Weightlifting has explosive force, survival training has movement over rough terrain, tennis has you moving quickly over the court... so of course it's traditional Athletics, it lets you dodge and run and climb.<br /> <br /> Yoga is stretching, rolling out your mat, doing some breathing exercises and assuming positions. It's Flexibility, yes, when you've warmed up. It's Precise Movements, yes, but only the ones you have practised and learnt to do. It's physical fitness... in the sense that when you get ready to DO YOGA you can, well, do yoga really well. Case in point, I know someone who would fall into a Yoga 2, Athletics 0 situation, being perfectly good at yoga (to the point where they could JUST pass for an instructor) but couldn't lift her own weight off the floor, do a pressup or run for more than about 30 seconds, let alone climb or dodge.<br /> <br /> American Football is not the same as Yoga. If someone came to me saying &quot;this is an American Football player who was embraced or awakened&quot;, and they had Strength 1, Dexterity 1, Stamina 1, Athletics 0, Brawl 0 and American Football 5, I would laugh at them. Logical consistency isn't the same as an EXP tax, and furthermore they will WANT to have decent physical stats anyways if they're playing an American Footballer. That's different to forcing the Yoga Master to have Athletics (Yoga is my focus but I have no interest in &quot;getting physical&quot;, I practise very slowly and the little fitness it gives me is represented by my Stamina) or forcing the Hacker to have Computer Science (I learnt Computing by teaching myself, I love hardware and software but I have no concept of the actual theory behind the software or hardware, I'm a Mechanic not an Engineer if you see what I mean).<br /> <br /> As for your hockey example, hitting someone in the face with a hockey stick isn't hockey, it's Melee. If you want to use Hockey to do Melee, you can do it at +2 difficulty. If someone said to me in character gen &quot;oh and he uses his focus stick as a weapon&quot; I would say &quot;take some Melee then, and I'll give you the Hockey dots a bit cheaper since I doubt it will come up in play that often&quot;.<br /> <br /> I see a difference between:<br /> <br /> &quot;I want to be an Athlete&quot; - &quot;Take some dots in these things that you will DEFINITELY USE then&quot;<br /> <br /> And...<br /> <br /> &quot;I want to be a Hacker&quot; - &quot;Take some dots in Computer Science that you will never use since Computer is the default roll for almost everything, just to justify your high rating in Computer&quot;<br /> <br /> And...<br /> <br /> &quot;I want to be a Yoga Master&quot; - &quot;Take some dots in things that you may or may not use, because learning Yoga will always make you physically fit, regardless of how you do it, and no, you can't represent that with higher Stamina or Dexterity or even Strength (your choice), it HAS to be Athletics because I say so.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T18:31:45-08:00","id":1048015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1047828#post1047828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Your physical fitness is a mixture of your Strength, Dexterity, Stamina and Athletics.</div> </div> </div> </div>...and here we go. You didn't actually read my post nearly as well as you thought you did. I addressed this exact point in it by pointing out the role Strength, Dexterity, and Stamina play in overall physical ability, <i>quite intentionally</i> pointing out Athletics rating is better-characterized as <i>physical conditioning</i> taking into account a person's natural ability. And guess what, people who are naturally very strong, agile, or tough aren't necessarily physically conditioned. I know people who could probably pick my 160-pound ass up and throw me around like a rag doll despite breaking out in a sweat at the very sight of a weight set...because they have a foot and a hundred pounds on me, not because they're more athletic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Athletics is a talent...since they have trained to move explosively... </div> </div> </div>Ah, the real issue raises its ugly head. Your personal definition of what does and does not constitute Athletics is <b><i>woefully </i></b>inadequate, if that's all you consider Athletics. Even weightlifters must have finely-honed kinesthetic and vestibular sense, for example, because they have to be able to maintain balance when lifting and compensate for losses of balance, otherwise -- hell, <i>because</i> -- things <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/BDg28psw1E8/\" target=\"_blank\">like this</a> happen. That, by the way, was a professional weightlifter having an &quot;oops&quot; moment, knowing she just had an &quot;oops&quot; moment, and bailing out before she got herself seriously injured, or even killed like <a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/early-lead/wp/2016/12/29/weightlifter-dies-after-315-pound-barbell-falls-on-his-neck-at-iowa-gym/?utm_term=.2cf5bac50a57\" target=\"_blank\">this poor guy</a>.<br /> <br /> But wait...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ... and carry their strength in precise ways. [...] All of the types of Athletics you're listing involve explosive movement which MUST improve your physical fitness and your ability to move quickly in demanding and varied ways. </div> </div> </div>Ah, so kinesthetic and vestibular sense, and spatial awareness, only counts when you say it does. Funny that, you seem to consider that in service to &quot;explosive&quot; or &quot;quick&quot; movement rather than a foundation of them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yoga is stretching, rolling out your mat, doing some breathing exercises and assuming positions. </div> </div> </div>Assuming positions <a href=\"http://yogaposeweekly.letsglo.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/07/image84.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">like this</a>. Or <a href=\"https://www.doyouyoga.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/01/How-To-Do-Yoga-Scorpion-Handstand-VIDEO.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>.Or <a href=\"http://yoga.top5.com/images/uploads/Large/b5e32301-92be-49e1-b7e2-bda35294385a.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>. You think you can even get into, let alone hold, advanced poses without the physical conditioning -- flexibility, balance, muscle memory, lung power, fatigue resistance -- to do it? And if you're doing any kind of hot yoga, the ability to do that while Not Getting Heat Stroke.<br /> <br /> And, you don't think that baseline physical conditioning won't impact a person's ability to move in &quot;explosive&quot; or &quot;quick&quot; ways?<br /> <br /> I'd wonder what someone who trains in and practices tai chi chuan, or qigong, as martial arts might say about all this. Because the modernized, popular, forms are based on <i>martial arts </i>training techniques to develop muscle memory (kinesthetic sense), balance (vestibular sense), and body strength sufficient to move onto more advanced material.<br /> <br /> Or, hell, for that matter, a pole dancer. They're a hell of a lot more common than people who are well-studied in Chinese martial arts or yogis, and the <i>exact same principles</i> where developing muscle memory, balance, and body strength are concerned, go into learning how to pole dance.<br /> <br /> Which, actually, what would <i>you </i>think about an exotic dancer PC (and if I remember right, &quot;stripper&quot; <i>is</i>a published Ecstatic archetype/character concept) who had zero dots in Athletics, especially considering that PC more likely than not is going to have their primaries in social opposed to physical? Is Art (dance) a panacea skill where this is concerned, too, or would you expect them to have dots in Athletics to reflect the level of physical conditioning it takes to pole dance?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Case in point, I know someone who would fall into a Yoga 2, Athletics 0 situation, being perfectly good at yoga (to the point where they could JUST pass for an instructor) but <i><b>couldn't lift her own weight off the floor, do a pressup</b></i> or run for more than about 30 seconds, let alone climb or dodge. </div> </div> </div>I'd actually go ask your friend, then, exactly what the hell yoga is, what goes into doing it, and what level of physical conditioning you have to have to do it, and while you're at it ask your friend to demonstrate for you a hand or shoulder stand or two, and a <a href=\"https://www.gaia.com/article/why-are-yoga-pushups-chaturanga-so-difficult\" target=\"_blank\">chaturanga dandasana</a>.<br /> <br /> Chaturanga, by the way, is a <b><i>basic</i></b> pose. It's Yoga 101, considering it's a part of Sun Salutation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and<i><b> they had Strength 1, Dexterity 1, Stamina 1</b></i>, Athletics 0, Brawl 0 and American Football 5... </div> </div> </div>Aww, you sneaky boy you. This is a &quot;red herring, <i>reductio ad absurdum</i>, and points preemptively addressed&quot;-free zone, thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 02-13-2017, 01:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T18:47:04-08:00","id":1048022,"title":"","content":"Question<br /> Would you be ok with Drive 4 including the ability to roll for basic car specific tech stuff without needing Technology?<br /> If we assume you are right and it wouldn't make sense to have this driving knowledge without this being in the mix, then why not just say it's covered in the Drive stat?<br /> Why should great drivers also have to know how microwaves work?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T05:49:58-08:00","id":1048202,"title":"","content":"Has anyone read the Cuckoo's Egg. It's the story of an American computer manager who figured out a Russian agent was using his lab's computers as a backdoor into secure government systems. It's a true story, too. <br /> <br /> I bring it up because there are two moments that seem to relate to this conversation.<br /> <br /> 1) He found out about the agent because one of the accounts was 75 cents off, but the lab only made purchases in tens or hundreds of dollars.<br /> <br /> 2) He would periodically stymie the hacker by isolating the computer he was routing through and shaking his keys over the physical network connection. <br /> <br /> Now I might file (1) under Computers, although I'd be more likely to call it Investigation. (2) is Computer Science all the way. While there is a good chance that anyone skilled with hacking would know enough to do that, but I could easily see someone too focused on the software side of things to think much about the hardware.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T13:16:14-08:00","id":1048403,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a> (1) I was building things up from the ground logically. I read what you wrote. I was just stating what I thought about it all.<br /> <br /> (2) Yes, those positions are complicated. Yes, you'd think that Yoga means &quot;able to do a pressup&quot;. Here's why you're wrong. My friend DOES have Yoga 2 and Athletics 0. If you ask her to do a Sun Salutation, she doesn't just drop on the ground and do it. Like most Yoga practitioners she warms up. So yes, you can be a Master of Yoga and not be able to do a press-up, situp or pull-up (what I meant by lifting your own weight) under organic, natural conditions. Vampire pushes you off the edge of a building? You can't pull yourself up with Yoga, you haven't done your breathing exercises or warmed up. Drill Seargant tells you to &quot;drop and give me 20!&quot;? 'Fraid all of that time at the community center focusing on your Chakras won't help you do random exercise outside of the specific Yoga-based environment. Yoga isn't random, get on with it exercise by default. For some people Yoga can be 0 to Complex Yoga Position in 10 seconds, but for someone with Yoga 5 and Athletics 0 (still possible, if unlikely) it would be 0 to light stretches to bigger stretches to short rest to serious stretches to simple positions to The Position You Were Going For.<br /> <br /> (3) The Martial Artist wouldn't feel like needing Athletics was a Tax, however, since anyone who is fighting in hand-to-hand combat will benefit from Athletics eventually. With the Dancer, I would have a hard time believing that Dance is purely Performance; my personal view is that Dance Moves are Athletics, Dancing to Impress An Audience is Performance. And you can't see why spinning around a pole at speed is different to spending 20 minutes breathing and then doing a few stretches which are grounded in Eastern Mysticism? Seriously? Would the Dancer feel taxed? Possibly. I would simply say that if Dance is your focus, Athletics will work for anything that isn't impressing an audience (so everything except Spirit and Mind), if you're using Spirit or Mind you'll roll Dexterity + Performance since in that case you're seeking to impress. I'd use the Complementary Rolls optional ruling, so if you get successes on the Athletics roll that will make the Performance roll easier, so I doubt they'll be complaining.<br /> <br /> (4) I'm stating what I would do if someone came to me with that build. I wasn't saying anything about your argument; I was rationalizing why I think an ST should sometimes demand certain dots be on the sheet.<br /> <br /> Overall I think everything should make sense, but it's important to avoid taxing. Taxing would be defined as &quot;here are some dots you will never use/need, but I think you need to have them to be allowed these dots you actually will use/need&quot;. Making the NFL Player take Athletics and Brawl isn't taxing since they will use them. Making the Occult Researcher take Academics (as well as Occult) isn't taxing since they will use it. Making the Yoga Master take Athletics? That COULD be taxing if their character was conceived a certain way, that is &quot;Master of Yoga As Focus, absolutely useless in combat, fighting/running/climbing is for other people&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T20:42:30-08:00","id":1048565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1048403#post1048403\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(1) I was building things up from the ground logically. I read what you wrote. I was just stating what I thought about it all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, at this point what's at heart here is beyond clear.<br /> <br /> 1. You actually have no idea how physically intense yoga actually is, and from that<br /> <br /> 2. You don't <i>personally</i> consider yoga an athletic pursuit.<br /> <br /> As someone who does yoga, I can only tell you that you are categorically incorrect, and based upon that misconception your opinion of it is entirely unfounded. Alas, it seems not only are you unwilling to budge on this misconception, you refuse to entertain the notion you may have misconceptions in the first place. From this, there's simply no point continuing this discussion, but I will say I hope in the future you educate yourself a bit, try it out even, and come back to this conversation in retrospect once you have corrected those misconceptions on your own time.<br /> <br /> That said, closing notes:<br /> <br /> So, another <i>ad hoc</i> justification for why yoga isn't athletic, is &quot;you have to warm up&quot;? By this logic, <i>no</i> sport or physical activity is athletic as <i>every </i>athlete, or even normal person before exercise (if they know how to actually exercise without incurring self-injury), warms up before physical activity. And even then, the more physically conditioned a person is, they'll have to warm up less and faster. And, if they for whatever reason <i>must </i>engage in physical activity, they can do more <i>without</i> warming up, and at lower risk of injury, due to that conditioning.<br /> <br /> And guess what, yogis are no different. I don't have the flexibility, strength, or balance to get into expert positions or do expert sequences, but I can do moderate-difficulty stuff after warming up, and indeed I <i>can</i> go from sitting to doing a Sun Salutation without warming up. Because I've been doing it long enough to have built the strength, flexibility, and balance.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, you still cling to the &quot;XP/dot tax&quot; despite conceding, here, the existence of the complimentary rolls rule. And ignoring the fact, as I stated earlier, you can't swing a dead cat in MtA without hitting an XP break for attributes, abilities, <i>even spheres</i>. Nor paying anything more than lip service to the multitudes of ways to maximize the impact of smaller dice pools, like the &quot;magick enhancing abilities&quot; rule. <i>All</i> of which trivialize this &quot;XP/dot tax&quot; about which you keep harping.<br /> <br /> Yet, you've conceded ST's are well within their rights to demand players redistribute dots if they've made unrealistic characters. Just not in this particular case, apparently, <i>in your personal opinion</i> based upon misconceptions I've (pun unintended) bent over backwards to point out. All to justify, and here's the punchline, a character concept -- a yogi that apparently does no other forms of exercise, yet somehow retains a couch potato physicality despite having a guru's expertise, and may or may not have physical primaries to somehow justify a couch potato physicality -- which is such a fringe case it falls squarely into the &quot;unrealistic character&quot; category.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-14T00:17:36-08:00","id":1048615,"title":"","content":"All I can say is that I have a friend who has what you could call Yoga 2, and a friend with what you could call Athletics 2, based on descriptions, with roughly equivalent 'physical stats'. The latter can do a press-up effortlessly without warning or a warm-up. The former needs to sit down and stretch. There is a fundamental difference, at least for some Yoga practitioners. You can posture and claim I don't know what I'm talking about, but it doesn't take a genius to see that knowledge and skill in Yoga doesn't necessarily have to come with rapid, in the moment, 3 second turns Athletics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-15T03:36:29-08:00","id":1049208,"title":"","content":"Sorry but arguing about dots in Athletics is pointless, almost every player character is going to have some dots in Athletics simply so that they can avoid the occasional attack. This is why Dodge and Athletics were rolled into one for the 20th anniversary editions - everyone takes Dodge and someone with a concept that includes Athletics is penalised for buying a skill that has limited application.<br /> <br /> For fairness sake we have to assume that each and every skill in the game should be worth as much as each other skill, otherwise the design of the game is broken (to a greater or lesser extent) and some players will end up with 'better' characters simply because their concept fits the 'best' skills. I'm excluding characters that are deliberately gimped of course.<br /> <br /> That assumption is why we call requiring buying one skill in order to have another a 'tax' - it's not fair if the Computer skill is used only for routine computing tasks and the Science (computer) knowledge is used for anything esoteric. Drive is used for routine driving tasks but requiring Knowledge (heavy goods vehicles) in order to drive trucks at all would be unfair whereas saying that Drive 4 with a specialisation in heavy goods vehicles is required to safely drive a truck is not (especially as you could probably get a truck moving even with Drive 1).<br /> <br /> Similarly the skill points costs is based on the same principle - having four dots in one skill costs more points so should be worth more than having two dots in two skills - it is unfair if Computer 2 with Science (computer) 2 at a total cost of 10 xp is more useful than Computer 4 at a cost of 15 xp.<br /> <br /> If you're requiring equal levels of Computer and Science (computer) to accomplish a given tasks then that effectively means Computer is a skill that costs twice as much as others! Is it twice as effective as others? If you're playing it as if it is then you're probably playing it against the intended design<br /> <br /> There are plenty of ways to give ridiculously specific examples that are totally unfair from one point of view but put simply: imposing a cost on a selected set of tasks within the game is unfair to the players wishing to attempt those tasks when other tasks are cheaper. By doing so you are indirectly forcing players into a certain playstyle. Especially when the selected tasks are based on personal bias.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11490,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","user":"Dogstar","date":". 02-15-2017, 06:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-15T12:37:14-08:00","id":1049427,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1044811-do-people-use-abilities-enhancing-magic-every-single-time-they-use-magic?p=1048565#post1048565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yet, you've conceded ST's are well within their rights to demand players redistribute dots if they've made unrealistic characters. Just not in this particular case, apparently, <i>in your personal opinion</i> based upon misconceptions I've (pun unintended) bent over backwards to point out. All to justify, and here's the punchline, a character concept -- a yogi that apparently does no other forms of exercise, yet somehow retains a couch potato physicality despite having a guru's expertise, and may or may not have physical primaries to somehow justify a couch potato physicality -- which is such a fringe case it falls squarely into the &quot;unrealistic character&quot; category.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, now, that's entirely realistic!<br /> <br /> They're clearly formerly an expendable purpose-built infiltrator construct designed to 'pass' in a class long enough to get alone with the teacher and assassinate them. They have Yoga 5, Athletics 0 because their designer downloaded the specialist knowledge but didn't have the funding to add anything else to the instinct package. And had very peculiar views on optimizing capabilities.<br /> <br /> See? Entirely reasonable in-character backstory that fits the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6072-quantumboost","name":"Quantumboost","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6072_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"590"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}